{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790538813,"nonce":"+vjLolG+kRsLWQC9RkS5vxzhBTQ=","title":"wasm: typed closures for foreign functions, through per-type wrappers from Ash","message":"On wasm a foreign function reaches Haxe as a var-args closure (callback::function_for, over hlp_make_var_args), because Ash's record closure needs fun_record, which captures argument registers and exists only on aarch64 and x86_64 SysV. Ash a077d38 makes hlp_alloc_record_closure return null elsewhere. The var-args closure's type never matches a call site's, so compiled Haxe takes its dynamic path: each argument boxed as a vdynamic, an array built, hlp_dyn_call, and the result unboxed.\n\nCost, caribou-interop's wasm bench (Mac, 200000 iterations, median of 5): Haxe→Wren closure call 478.9 ns, against 19.8 ns for a Haxe→Haxe closure and 23.3 ns for a linked Haxe→Wren static call.\n\nThe route to a typed closure is what HashLink/C does with hlc_get_wrapper: the compiler emits one wrapper per function type in the program, of exactly that wasm signature, which packs its arguments and calls one runtime entry with the closure's bound value. Ash already emits one trampoline per signature for the other direction (crates/ash/src/llvm/aot_trampoline.rs, keyed by the same hl_type key), and std/src/fun.rs has the hlc_get_wrapper hook with no generator behind it. With wrappers, callback::function_for_typed would make a closure of the program's type whose fun is the wrapper for that type, and compiled Haxe would call it directly. Needs Ash (ash-b7) for the generator; caribou's side is the entry the wrapper calls and choosing the wrapper in function_for_typed.","files":null}]}